# Environment configuration template. Copy to `.env.local` and fill in real values.
#
# Never commit a populated `.env`/`.env.local`. Only this template is tracked.
#
# `SUPABASE_ANON_KEY` is public by design: it is the publishable key that ships to the
# browser. `SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY` bypasses Row Level Security and MUST stay server-side.
#
# There are SEVEN variables, in three groups, not three. The three server variables below are what
# the server-side and privileged Supabase clients read (`@/lib/env/server`). The two
# `NEXT_PUBLIC_*` variables are what the browser client reads (`@/lib/env/client`),
# and they are a separate pair of names rather than a re-export, because a `NEXT_PUBLIC_*`
# value is inlined into the client bundle at build time and the service-role key must
# never be in that bundle. Declaring the server names alone produces a `ClientEnvError`
# naming two variables this template never mentions.
#
# All five of those carry the SAME values in a real project — the Supabase project URL and its
# publishable anon key. They are separate names only because of the bundle boundary.
#
# The two `ADMIN_*` variables are a separate concern from Supabase entirely, and they are validated
# separately: the public validator routes never read them, so leaving them unset does not break the
# public site. That separation is deliberate — the alternative makes every public request fail in any
# environment that has not configured a researcher credential.

# --- Browser-safe. Inlined into the client bundle; these are not secrets. ---------
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL="https://your-project-ref.supabase.co"
NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY="your-publishable-anon-key"

# --- Server-side. Never exposed to the browser. -----------------------------------
SUPABASE_URL="https://your-project-ref.supabase.co"
SUPABASE_ANON_KEY="your-publishable-anon-key"
SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY="your-service-role-key-do-not-commit"

# --- Researcher access. Server-side only; never give either one a NEXT_PUBLIC_ name. ---
#
# `ADMIN_OPERATOR_SECRETS` is a comma-separated list of the operator credentials the research
# team signs in with. One entry per person, so that revoking one person's access does not sign
# everyone out.
#
#   - Generate a real one with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('base64url'))"
#   - Each entry must be non-empty. A blank entry is rejected, and a value that is present but
#     empty or only whitespace is treated as NOT configured at all.
#   - REORDERING this list signs out everyone whose session is still open. A session records which
#     position signed it in, so that one person's access can be revoked on its own; moving the
#     entries around moves those positions. This is the one genuinely surprising consequence of
#     the format, which is why it is here and not only in the spec.
#
# `ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET` protects the integrity of an open researcher session. It must NOT be the
# same value as any operator credential: it is the key that forges sessions, so a credential that
# doubles as this key would turn any captured session into a credential. Generate it separately.
#
# THE TWO VALUES BELOW ARE DELIBERATELY EMPTY, AND THAT IS NOT A PLACEHOLDER TO FILL IN WITH SAMPLE
# TEXT. The Supabase keys above carry fake values because a fake project reference cannot connect to
# anything, so the risk of shipping one is nil. An operator credential has no such property: a
# published sample value is a perfectly valid credential, so "placeholder-operator-token" in a
# deployed environment would authorize anyone who has read this template, which is in the repository.
# Leaving these empty means the admin area refuses every request, and a real value has to be
# deliberately generated and deliberately pasted.
ADMIN_OPERATOR_SECRETS=""
ADMIN_SESSION_SECRET=""